---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';

const openapi = String.raw`openapi: 3.1.0
info:
  title: Accounts
  version: 1.4.0
paths:
  /accounts/{id}/withdrawals:
    post:
      operationId: withdraw
      parameters:
        - name: id
          in: path
          required: true
          schema: { type: string, format: uuid }
      requestBody:
        required: true
        content:
          application/json:
            schema:
              type: object
              required: [amount, currency]
              properties:
                amount: { type: integer, minimum: 1 }
                currency: { type: string, enum: [BRL, USD] }
      responses:
        "201":
          description: Withdrawal accepted
        "409":
          description: Insufficient funds
          content:
            application/json:
              schema: { $ref: "#/components/schemas/Problem" }`;

const phpContract = String.raw`namespace App\Domain\Account;

final class Withdrawal
{
    private function __construct(
        private AccountId $account,
        private Money $amount,
    ) {}

    public static function fromRequest(AccountId $account, Money $amount): self
    {
        if ($amount->isNegative()) {
            throw new InvalidWithdrawalAmount($amount);
        }

        return new self($account, $amount);
    }

    public function account(): AccountId { return $this->account; }
    public function amount(): Money { return $this->amount; }
}`;

const csContract = String.raw`namespace App.Domain.Account;

public sealed record Withdrawal(AccountId Account, Money Amount)
{
    public static Withdrawal FromRequest(AccountId account, Money amount)
    {
        if (amount.IsNegative)
            throw new InvalidWithdrawalAmount(amount);

        return new Withdrawal(account, amount);
    }
}`;
---

<Doc
  title="Contracts and language"
  kicker="Track U — Universal Language & Contracts"
  description="Ubiquitous language, design-first contracts and compatibility rules that make a promise testable."
  path="/guide/contracts"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Two systems integrate at a contract, not at a class. The contract is the part you
    cannot change casually, and the language inside it is the part your business
    expert must recognize.
  </p>

  <h2>Ubiquitous language</h2>

  <p>
    If the expert says “settlement”, the code does not say
    <code>PaymentResult</code>. Names are not cosmetic: a wrong name forces a
    translation layer in someone’s head, and that layer is where requirements get
    lost.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Business says</th><th>Code says</th><th>Why it hurts</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>settlement</td><td><code>PaymentResult</code></td><td>Two names for one concept; reports and code disagree</td></tr>
        <tr><td>chargeback</td><td><code>refund</code></td><td>Different lifecycles collapsed into one type</td></tr>
        <tr><td>holder</td><td><code>user</code></td><td>“User” leaks a technical actor into a financial rule</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The test is mechanical: read a use case aloud to the expert. When they interrupt to
    correct a word, the code is wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Design first</h2>

  <p>
    The contract is written before the implementation. It is the artifact two teams
    can disagree about cheaply, and the one that survives a rewrite.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="OpenAPI 3.1 — the promise"
    tabs={[{ id: 'php', label: 'openapi.yaml', code: openapi }]}
  />

  <p>
    Note what the contract does <strong>not</strong> contain: class names, database
    columns, framework annotations. It describes resources, verbs and failure modes —
    the same vocabulary the business uses.
  </p>

  <h2>The contract inside the code</h2>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Validation belongs to the concept"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpContract },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C#', code: csContract },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The request object is not a DTO bag. It is the smallest type that can represent a
    valid withdrawal — and the only place where “amount must be positive” is allowed to
    live.
  </p>

  <h2>Compatibility rules</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Additive only.</strong> New optional fields and new endpoints are safe; renaming and removal are breaking.</li>
    <li><strong>Never reuse a field name</strong> with a different meaning. That is a silent breaking change.</li>
    <li><strong>Version the contract, not the class.</strong> A semantic version on the API, not on an internal namespace.</li>
    <li><strong>Deprecate before deleting.</strong> Announce, warn in responses, then remove — in that order.</li>
    <li><strong>Error shape is part of the contract.</strong> A <code>409</code> with an undocumented body is a broken promise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Test the promise, not the implementation</h2>

  <p>
    Contract tests validate that the implementation still honours the document:
    schema validation of responses, consumer-driven tests (Pact), and a CI step that
    diffs the published spec against the previous version and fails on breaking
    changes.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Contract tooling</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td>OpenAPI validators, <code>league/openapi-psr7-validator</code>, Pact PHP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td>NSwag, Microsoft.OpenApi, Pact .NET</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td>springdoc-openapi, Pact JVM</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>spec validation in integration tests, Pact via a binding</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Note label="The rule" variant="rule">
    <p>
      If the code does not read like the business expert speaks, or if the contract is
      bypassed, the system is broken.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>Bypassing is the default failure</h2>

  <p>
    Contracts are usually not broken by bad design — they are bypassed by convenience:
    a service calling another’s database, a client reading an internal endpoint, a
    “temporary” field added straight in production. Each bypass is a second source of
    truth, and two sources of truth never stay equal for long.
  </p>

  <p>
    The countermeasure is social before technical: the contract must be the only door,
    and the door must be cheaper to use than the wall.
  </p>
</Doc>
